        "dependency_level": 5,
        "statement": "Assume full AC for the library Jones invariant. An oriented link is a finite disjoint union of oriented smooth circles embedded in $S^3$, considered up to ambient isotopy; an oriented classical knot is the one-component case. For an oriented link $L\\subset S^3$, let $V_L(x)\\in\\mathbb Z[x^{\\pm1}]$ be the library-normalized Jones polynomial, with $V_U(x)=1$ for the unknot $U$; write $V_K$ for its value on a knot $K$. Here $x=t^{1/2}$ in the library convention $t=A^{-4}$, so an oriented skein triple satisfies $$x^{-2}V_{L_+}(x)-x^2V_{L_-}(x)=(x-x^{-1})V_{L_0}(x).$$ Set $p=x-x^{-1}$. By the unique Laurent decomposition, write $$V_K(x)=\\sum_{q\\ge0}(a_q(K)+b_q(K)x)p^q,$$ where $a_q(K),b_q(K)\\in\\mathbb Z$ and only finitely many are nonzero. The **Jones–Vassiliev coefficient vector of order $q$** is $$f_q(K):=(a_q(K),b_q(K))^{\\mathsf T},$$ and $c_q(K):=a_q(K)+b_q(K)$. This is the algebraic coefficient-vector definition used by Carmi and Cohen. The claim that the coordinates of $f_q$ are finite-type invariants of order at most $q$ is a separate theorem and is not part of this definition.",
