        "statement": "Assume full AC for the library Jones invariant and $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the crossing number. A classical knot is a smooth embedding $S^1\\hookrightarrow S^3$ considered up to ambient isotopy; $U$ is the unknot represented by a crossing-free circle, and “nontrivial” means it is not ambient isotopic to $U$. A regular diagram is a generic plane projection with only transverse double points, each marked with over/under information. Let $c(K)$ be the least crossing count among regular diagrams representing $K$. Let $V_K(x)$ be the library-normalized Jones polynomial with $V_U(x)=1$, where $x=t^{1/2}$ and $t=A^{-4}$. Set $p=x-x^{-1}$ and write the unique expansion $$V_K(x)=\\sum_{q\\ge0}(a_q(K)+b_q(K)x)p^q,$$ where the integer coefficients have finite support; set $f_q(K)=(a_q(K),b_q(K))^{\\mathsf T}$. The historical Jones unknot-detection question is whether $V_K(x)=1$ implies $K=U$. Roberts's 1999 notes record the implication as a conjecture. Carmi and Cohen's arXiv:2606.22410v1, submitted on 2026-06-21, claims that every nontrivial classical knot has a nonzero coefficient vector $f_q(K)$ for some integer $q$ with $2\\le q\\le3c(K)$, and claims $V_K(x)=1\\Rightarrow K=U$. This scaffold has read the stated result and the paper's proof route but has not independently validated the claimed proof; it asserts neither proof acceptance nor a definitive current open status. The source's machine argument still requires an independent diagram-level audit of Proposition 5.12, particularly its exceptional-state collapse, smoothing proxy, and two-machine locality. Its numerical estimate $B_{\\rm aux}\\le B_{\\rm host}+12$ is consistent with the stated overhead $\\kappa_{\\rm mach}=11$: the argument uses the next layer $r+1$ after $r>B_{\\rm host}+11$. This arithmetic check does not verify the geometric reductions. No Jones-detection theorem is registered by this scaffold.",
